        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 495581),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 507409),
          text:
              '"You could tell them what really happened," Imaan said gently. "You didn\'t get that job because you were not respected. You chose to do your best even when you were given a job nobody wanted."',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 507409),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 517826),
          text:
              'Rashid looked down. "Would it even matter? Hamid has already got twenty-two votes.” Imaan was quiet for a moment. She did not have an easy answer.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 518217),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 537477),
          text:
              'Imaan shook her head. "Respecting an election does not mean pretending every concern is unimportant. If you lose, accept the result without insulting Hamid. But if a Captain misuses the role, you can still speak respectfully to Miss and explain what is happening."',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 537477),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 552677),
          text:
              'Rashid nodded. "That is what worries me. It is not only the title. Hamid sometimes laughs at people, makes promises he cannot keep and enjoys being the centre of attention. A leader\'s behaviour can affect the whole class."',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 553091),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 568708),
          text:
              'Imaan replied, "Then focus on what you can control: tell the truth, follow the rules, and speak through the right channel if something becomes unfair. Respect is not silence. It is choosing a respectful way to act."',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 568708),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 575161),
          text:
              'That evening, another folded note appeared on the school noticeboard.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 576613),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 583173),
          text:
              '"A kind act done quietly is never wasted, even when no one claps."',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 583173),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 591426),
          text:
              'Rashid read the message twice. In the lower corner was the same tiny green dot.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 591426),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 603267),
          text:
              'His curiosity sharpened. The writer had not only seen him at the gate; this person seemed to know what was happening inside school.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 603267),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 632589),
          text:
              'Rashid looked down the empty corridor. For the first time, the notes felt less like random advice and more like a trail.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 632589),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 632590),
          text: 'Challenge of the Day 3: The Unseen Helper',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 632590),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 652557),
          text:
              'Mission: Choose one useful job at school that people usually avoid – arranging chairs, picking up litter, returning books, helping organise materials – and do it without announcing it.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 652557),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 652558),
          text:
              'Level-Up: Ages 9-12: Do the task without posting, boasting or asking someone to notice. Later, write one sentence about how the work helped others.',
        ),
      ],
    ),
    Story(
      id: 'respect_en_04',
      title: 'Ep 4 · The Courage to Speak',
      narrator: 'Imaan & Akhlaq',
      coverAsset: 'assets/covers/respect.webp',
      audioAsset: 'assets/audio/en/respect/04_the_courage_to_speak.ogg',
      category: StoryCategory.moral,
      description: 'Respect — episode 4 of 10.',
      captions: [
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 3846),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 3847),
          text: 'Chapter Four: The Courage to Speak',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 3847),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 21480),
          text:
              'Monday morning began like any other school day. Rashid was walking towards his classroom when he heard laughter behind the library entrance. Hamid stood with two other boys in front of a younger student from Class Three, who held his lunchbox tightly against his chest.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 21480),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 37648),
          text:
              'Hamid pointed at the boy’s old, dusty shoes. “Did you find those on the road?” The others laughed. The younger boy looked down. Rashid stopped. He remembered the rumours about himself and the mysterious notes about respect.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 38608),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 63776),
          text:
              'Earlier that morning, Rashid had asked Sheikh Uncle a difficult question: “What do you do when someone is being disrespectful, but you do not want to become disrespectful yourself?” Sheikh Uncle had replied, “Respect does not mean staying silent when someone is being humiliated. Speak calmly, protect the person being hurt, and do not become cruel while correcting cruelty.”',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 64557),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 83584),
          text:
              'Now Rashid stepped forward. “That is enough.” Hamid turned. “Why? We are only joking.” Rashid looked at the younger boy. “He is not laughing.” Then he faced Hamid again. “If you want to lead people, they should feel safer around you, not smaller.”',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 84319),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 97292),
          text:
              'Hamid’s face changed. One of his friends tried to pull him away, but Rashid did not insult them or raise his voice. After a tense moment, Hamid muttered, “Whatever,” and the group walked away.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 97696),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 123240),
          text:
              'Imaan and Akhlaaq arrived as the younger boy was picking up his lunchbox. Imaan asked his name and whether he wanted someone to walk with him to class. Akhlaaq picked up the lunchbox and handed it back. A joke about Hamid’s “comedy career” formed in his mind, but he let it go. If they had just stopped one child being humiliated, turning Hamid into the next joke would miss the lesson.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 124833),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 142888),
          text:
              '“JazakAllahu Khaira,” the younger boy said quietly. “You are welcome,” Rashid replied. When he had gone, Imaan looked at Rashid. “You spoke up without making Hamid the next target.” Akhlaaq nodded. “And I nearly did make him the next target. I think I understand the difference now.”',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 143616),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 170064),
          text:
              'The next morning, Rashid found a folded note tucked beneath the corner of the school noticeboard. Imaan and Akhlaaq leaned closer as he opened it: “Respect is not only shown in the words you speak. Sometimes it is shown in the courage to speak when someone else cannot.” The familiar green dot sat below the message.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 170064),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 185552),
          text:
              'Enough time had passed for the story behind the library to reach other people, but Rashid’s curiosity still sharpened. Whoever wrote the notes was paying close attention to what happened around the school.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 185552),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 191552),
          text: 'Challenge of the Day 4: Speak Up Without Putting Down',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 192775),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 210000),
          text:
              'Mission: If someone is being teased or excluded, use a calm sentence such as, \'That is not kind,\' \'Let us give them a chance,\' or \'Please stop.\' If the situation feels unsafe, get a teacher or trusted adult.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 211520),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 220588),
          text:
              'Level-Up: Ages 9-12: Practise disagreeing with a friend without insulting, mocking or embarrassing them.',
        ),
      ],
    ),
    Story(
      id: 'respect_en_05',
      title: 'Ep 5 · The Boy Behind the Badge',
      narrator: 'Imaan & Akhlaq',
      coverAsset: 'assets/covers/respect.webp',
      audioAsset: 'assets/audio/en/respect/05_the_boy_behind_the_badge.ogg',
      category: StoryCategory.moral,
      description: 'Respect — episode 5 of 10.',
      captions: [
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 30),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 2456),
          text: 'Chapter Five: The Boy Behind the Badge',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 2900),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 26628),
          text:
              'The morning of the final election felt different. Hamid arrived early wearing his brightest smile. Across the room, Rashid arranged his books while Imaan and Akhlaaq joined him. “You look calm,” Akhlaaq said. Rashid shrugged. “I still think Hamid has a lot to learn. But if he wins fairly, I will respect the result.”',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 26628),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 40548),
          text:
              'Miss entered and announced the final count. “Your new Class Captain is... Hamid.” The room burst into applause. Hamid’s friend jumped up as though they had won a cricket final.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 41101),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 62596),
          text:
              'Akhlaaq leaned towards Imaan. “After all those impossible promises, he still won?” His voice was louder than he intended. Imaan looked at him. Akhlaaq stopped. The unfair promises had been withdrawn and the final vote had followed the rules. Disliking the result did not give him permission to insult it.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 62804),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 85672),
          text:
              'Rashid stood first and offered Hamid his hand. “Congratulations.” Hamid looked surprised, then shook it. Akhlaaq came next. “Congratulations. I did not vote for you, but you won fairly.” Hamid laughed. “That may be the strangest congratulations I have received.” “It is the most honest one I have,” Akhlaaq replied.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 86312),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 96672),
          text:
              'Imaan smiled. “Now the harder part begins.” Hamid lifted his chin. “Being Captain?” “Being worthy of the responsibility,” she replied.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 96672),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 116000),
          text:
              'Later, Hamid stood near the classroom door wearing the Captain badge. Younger students greeted him, and his friend whispered, “You see? They already respect you.” Hamid liked the sound of that. In his mind, respect, attention and importance began to feel almost the same.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 116000),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 142388),
          text:
              'A strange voice came from beside the school wall. “Respect?” Hamid turned and saw a short, oddly dressed figure with a crooked smile. It was Faasid. “I was only admiring your little crown,” Faasid said, pointing at the badge. “It is not a crown,” Hamid replied. “Of course not,” Faasid said. “But people listen to you now. That must mean you are more important than before.”',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 142388),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 164644),
          text:
              'Hamid looked at the badge. “I won.” Faasid smiled. “Exactly. Perhaps you should not have to listen as much as everyone else.” The idea was unfair, but it was exactly what Hamid wanted to hear. Faasid tapped the badge. “If you are Captain, people should listen when you speak.” Hamid nodded slowly.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 164644),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 181644),
          text:
              'Later that day, Miss gave Hamid his first responsibility: organise the class for Spring Fair preparation. Hamid turned to the room. “Since I am Captain, I will decide what everyone is doing.” Rashid raised his hand. “Should we not discuss it together?”',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 182612),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 197492),
          text:
              'Hamid frowned. “I have already decided.” Akhlaaq said, “A good Captain listens to the people he leads.” Imaan added, “Winning gave you responsibility, Hamid. It did not make your classmates less important.”',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 197492),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 211860),
          text:
              'Hamid wanted to argue, but he remembered that the whole class was watching. “Fine. I will listen.” The students began sharing ideas. He listened, although every few moments his fingers returned to the badge.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 211860),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 233168),
          text:
              'After school, Rashid, Imaan and Akhlaaq walked towards the gate. Rashid admitted that congratulating Hamid had not been easy. Akhlaaq said, “It was not easy for me either. I wanted to complain about the vote before I remembered that respect also includes losing properly.”',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 233523),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 250516),
          text:
              'Imaan nodded. “And respecting the result does not mean we stay silent if Hamid later misuses the role. We can speak through the proper channel without insulting him.” Rashid smiled. “So respect is not agreement, and it is not silence.”',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 250516),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 265084),
          text:
              'Near the gate, another folded note waited in a crack in the wall: “A title may make people notice you. Only good character can make their respect last.” The green dot was there again.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 265084),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 274684),
          text:
              'Rashid looked through the classroom window at Hamid touching his badge. The election was over, but the real lesson had only begun.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 275684),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 279884),
          text: 'Challenge of the Day 5: Respect the Result',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 282452),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 297452),
          text:
              'Mission: During a game, class vote or group decision, practise being respectful when your choice does not win. Congratulate the winner or support the group decision without pretending you have no feelings.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 297452),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 307020),
          text:
              'Level-Up: Ages 9-12: If you disagree, state your concern through the proper channel instead of spreading complaints or rumours.',
        ),
      ],
    ),
    Story(
      id: 'respect_en_06',
      title: 'Ep 6 · The Captain\'s Special Seat',
      narrator: 'Imaan & Akhlaq',
      coverAsset: 'assets/covers/respect.webp',
      audioAsset: 'assets/audio/en/respect/06_the_captains_special_seat.ogg',
      category: StoryCategory.moral,
      description: 'Respect — episode 6 of 10.',
      captions: [
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 0),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 3715),
          text: 'Chapter Six: The Captain\'s Special Seat',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 3715),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 34283),
          text:
              'Hamid had been class captain for only three days, but he was already beginning to enjoy the position a little too much. He liked the way everyone called him "Captain Hamid." He liked standing at the front of the line and carrying the class register under his arm, even when there was no real reason to carry it. Most of all, he liked the feeling of being important. On Tuesday morning, Hamid walked into the classroom with his friend beside him and announced, "Captain coming through!"',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 34651),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 44291),
          text:
              'His friend immediately moved aside with a dramatic bow. "Make way! Make way! The most important person of the class has arrived!"',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 44291),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 48091),
          text: 'Hamid laughed, but secretly, he liked hearing it.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 48091),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 59619),
          text:
              'Across the room, Rashid, Imaan, and Akhlaaq exchanged a quick glance. Hamid said, taking his seat proudly. "I have important captain work to do."',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 59619),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 63619),
          text: 'Rashid looked at his empty desk. "What important work?"',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 63619),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 67819),
          text: 'Hamid leaned back. "Making sure everyone behaves."',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 68251),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 71419),
          text: 'Akhlaaq looked around the classroom. "You\'re sitting down."',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 71419),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 72779),
          text: '"Exactly."',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 72779),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 73163),
          text: '"And?"',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 73163),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 73419),
          text: '"I\'m supervising."',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 75302),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 79087),
          text: 'Akhlaaq raised an eyebrow. "From your chair?"',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 79087),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 84327),
          text:
              '"Leadership is about knowing when to assign work," Hamid replied confidently.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 84327),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 92087),
          text:
              'Later that morning, Faasid appeared near the corridor window, wearing the same crooked smile Hamid remembered.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 92087),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 102635),
          text:
              '“Very clever, Hamid,” Faasid said where Hamid could hear him. “Why do the hard work yourself when you can make everyone else do it?”',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 102635),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 108515),
          text:
              'Hamid heard him clearly. The suggestion pleased him more than he wanted to admit.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 108515),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 137771),
          text:
              'As the week continued, Hamid discovered that being captain came with many advantages. When Miss asked someone to collect the notebooks, Hamid pointed toward another student. When the class needed to arrange chairs for the Spring Fair, he gave everyone instructions but disappeared when the actual work began. And whenever there was a messy job that nobody wanted, Hamid suddenly remembered that he had "captain duties" somewhere else.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 137771),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 154651),
          text:
              'One afternoon, the teacher looked around the classroom after Spring Fair preparations. Papers were scattered across the floor, coloured paper was stuck beneath desks and empty glue bottles had been left on tables. “Who is going to help clean this up?” she asked.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 155191),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 179867),
          text:
              'Hamid raised his hand at once. “Rashid is good at cleaning. He can do it.” Miss looked at him. “Why Rashid alone?” Hamid hesitated. “Because his group did it well before.” Miss shook her head. “The whole group helped create this mess, so the whole group will help clear it. A Captain should be willing to begin the work, not use the badge to escape it.”',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 179867),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 204875),
          text:
              'Hamid’s face tightened. He did not like being corrected in front of everyone. Imaan noticed that expression because she remembered her own mistake with the campaign poster. She did not add another public lecture. Instead, she picked up a stack of paper and said, “Come on. We can finish faster if all of us start.” Akhlaaq handed Hamid a rubbish bag without comment. After a moment, Hamid took it.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 205515),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 229243),
          text:
              'Later, during reading time, a boy placed a box of biscuits beside his favourite book. Hamid pointed at the box. “Since I am Captain, I think I should get one.” The boy hesitated. “My mother packed them for me.” Hamid tapped his badge. “Come on. You should respect the Captain.” The boy slowly offered the box, and Hamid reached for the biggest biscuit.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 229637),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 242131),
          text:
              'Rashid stepped forward. “He looks as though he feels he cannot say no.” Akhlaaq added, “A title should never become pressure. Respecting your position does not mean giving you whatever you want.”',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 242451),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 268579),
          text:
              'Imaan wanted to correct Hamid immediately in front of everyone. She stopped herself. “Hamid, can I speak to you for a minute?” she asked. Near the doorway, away from the class, she said, “If someone gives because they are afraid to refuse you, that is not respect. It is pressure.” Hamid glanced back at the boy. “It is only a biscuit.” “Small choices show us what we think power is for,” Imaan replied.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 268579),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 284912),
          text:
              'Hamid returned to the boy and put the biscuit back. “Sorry. I should have asked instead of making the badge part of it.” The boy relaxed. “You can have one if you want.” Hamid chose a smaller biscuit this time. “JazakAllahu Khaira.” The boy smiled. “You’re welcome.”',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 284912),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 301875),
          text:
              'As they left school, Akhlaaq said quietly to Imaan, “You corrected him differently today.” Imaan nodded. “The message still mattered. I just did not need an audience.” Rashid smiled. “That may be one of the most respectful things any of us did today.”',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 302282),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 328283),
          text:
              'Faasid was waiting near the side gate. This time Hamid saw him clearly. “You gave the biscuit back?” Faasid asked with disgust. “What is the point of being important if you cannot enjoy the benefits?” Hamid looked at his badge. The old answer came easily: Because I am Captain. But for the first time, it did not sound as impressive in his own mind.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 328283),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 352931),
          text:
              'The next morning, Miss asked everyone to bring a favourite book for a reading activity. Hamid reached for another student’s book before asking. The boy pulled it back. Hamid paused. Before anyone corrected him, he said, “Sorry. May I look at it when you are finished?” Imaan glanced at Rashid. The change was tiny, but it had happened before a lecture.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 354139),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 378107),
          text:
              'Near the school gate that afternoon, Rashid found another folded note: “Respect begins when we remember that every heart wants to be treated with kindness.” The tiny green dot was there again. Rashid held it beside the others he had saved. Same paper. Same careful fold. Same green mark.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 378107),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 382107),
          text: 'Challenge of the Day 6: No Special Privileges',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 383607),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 399875),
          text:
              'Mission: In a group activity today, do not use age, popularity, a title or friendship to take the first turn, biggest share or easiest job. Ask before taking something that belongs to someone else.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 414043),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 414590),
          text:
              'Level-Up: Ages 9-12: Notice one situation where power or popularity could pressure another person. Choose consent and fairness instead.',
        ),
      ],
    ),
    Story(
      id: 'respect_en_07',
      title: 'Ep 7 · The Captain Learns to Listen',
      narrator: 'Imaan & Akhlaq',
      coverAsset: 'assets/covers/respect.webp',
      audioAsset: 'assets/audio/en/respect/07_the_captain_learns_to_listen.ogg',
      category: StoryCategory.moral,
      description: 'Respect — episode 7 of 10.',
      captions: [
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 329),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 4333),
          text: 'Chapter Seven: The Captain Learns to Listen',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 4333),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 24061),
          text:
              'The next morning, something unusual happened in class. Hamid arrived early carrying a stack of homework notebooks. Rashid stared. “What are those?” “The notebooks Miss asked me to collect,” Hamid replied. Akhlaaq raised an eyebrow. “And you actually collected them yourself?”',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 24701),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 47229),
          text:
              'Hamid gave him a look. “Why does everyone sound so surprised?” Imaan smiled. “Because you usually give the job to someone else.” Hamid opened his mouth to argue, then stopped. “Well... maybe I should not always do that.” Rashid smiled. It was a small change, but it was a change.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 47229),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 74797),
          text:
              'That same morning, Miss began explaining how the class labels for the Spring Fair had to be sorted. Akhlaaq heard the first instruction and immediately interrupted. “I know, Miss. Red labels for games, blue for books.” He grabbed the stack before she had finished. “Actually,” Miss said, “blue is for food stalls this year.” Akhlaaq froze with half the labels already placed on the wrong table.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 74797),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 99736),
          text:
              'A few children laughed. Akhlaaq normally would have rescued himself with a joke, but he knew exactly what had caused the mistake. He had listened only until he thought he knew the rest. Hamid, of all people, quietly helped him move the labels back. “Maybe let Miss finish first,” he said. Akhlaaq sighed. “I deserved that.”',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 99736),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 126941),
          text:
              'During break, Imaan teased him gently. “The great Akhlaaq defeated by one unfinished sentence.” He smiled, then became serious. “I think I interrupt people more than I notice. Sometimes I am already preparing my answer while they are still speaking.” Rashid nodded. “Listening is one of the ways we tell someone their words matter.”',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 126941),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 151969),
          text:
              'For the next few days, both Hamid and Akhlaaq practised in different ways. Hamid helped arrange chairs, collected classwork and stayed behind to clean the board. Akhlaaq began waiting an extra second before replying, especially when younger students spoke. He discovered that several conversations became easier when he stopped trying to be the quickest voice in them.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 151969),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 178717),
          text:
              'One afternoon, a younger boy struggled with a heavy box of books. Hamid moved to help, but Akhlaaq reached him first. “Where do you need these?” he asked. The boy began explaining. Akhlaaq almost interrupted with his own guess, then waited. “Library, second shelf,” the boy finished. “Got it,” Akhlaaq said. Hamid lifted the other side of the box. Neither boy needed to be told.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 179165),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 204013),
          text:
              'Faasid appeared beside Hamid near the playground. “Why are you carrying books for everyone else?” he asked. “You are the Captain. Let them serve you.” Hamid heard every word. He looked towards Akhlaaq, who was listening patiently while the younger boy explained where the box belonged. “Maybe a Captain should be useful,” Hamid replied. Faasid’s smile weakened.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 204013),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 225101),
          text:
              'That evening, Rashid sat with his grandmother and told her about the changes at school. “Hamid is learning to serve, and Akhlaaq is learning to listen,” he said. His grandmother laughed softly. “Then perhaps both boys are discovering that respect asks us to make room for other people.”',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 225101),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 257069),
          text:
              'The next morning, another folded note waited at the noticeboard. “A respectful heart does not become smaller when it gives others their place. It becomes greater.” Rashid checked the lower corner. Green dot. Across the entrance, Hamid held the door for a younger student while Akhlaaq listened to Ayaan explain something about the cricket team without interrupting once.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 259037),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 263037),
          text: 'Challenge of the Day 7: Family Listening Challenge',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 264227),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 277837),
          text:
              'Mission: At home, ask one family member, \'How was your day?\' Listen for two full minutes without interrupting. Then tell them one thing you heard so they know you were really listening.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 279005),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 287365),
          text:
              'Level-Up: Ages 9-12: Ask one follow-up question instead of immediately giving advice or changing the subject.',
        ),
      ],
    ),
    Story(
      id: 'respect_en_08',
      title: 'Ep 8 · The Test of True Respect',
      narrator: 'Imaan & Akhlaq',
      coverAsset: 'assets/covers/respect.webp',
      audioAsset: 'assets/audio/en/respect/08_the_test_of_true_respect.ogg',
      category: StoryCategory.moral,
      description: 'Respect — episode 8 of 10.',
      captions: [
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 0),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 2978),
          text: 'Chapter Eight: The Test of True Respect',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 2978),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 18066),
          text:
              'By the following week, Hamid had changed enough for everyone to notice. He still liked being called Captain Hamid and still enjoyed attention, but he was beginning to catch himself before turning the badge into a privilege.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 18066),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 29306),
          text:
              'One afternoon, Miss announced that the class would choose three students to represent them at the Spring Fair. Hamid immediately named his closest friend. “He is my friend.”',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 29306),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 54962),
          text:
              'Before anyone argued, Imaan raised her hand. “Could we agree on the reasons for choosing first?” Miss nodded. Imaan wrote three words on the board: effort, responsibility and suitability. Akhlaaq added, “And we use the same reasons for everyone, including our own friends.” Rashid looked at Hamid. “That makes the decision about the work, not about who we like most.”',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 70930),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 71166),
          text:
              'Faasid waited near the corridor after class and caught Hamid alone. “Choose your friend,” he urged. “That is what powerful people do.” Hamid heard him clearly. For a moment, the old temptation returned. His friend expected the favour.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 71560),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 86609),
          text:
              'When the class met again, Hamid looked at the names and then at the agreed criteria. “Ayaan has worked hard on the fair preparations,” he said. “He has been responsible, and he knows the activity well. I think he should represent us.”',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 86609),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 104458),
          text:
              'His friend stared at him. “But I’m your friend!” Hamid took a breath. “I know. Being my friend does not automatically make you the best person for every job.” The room became quiet. Akhlaaq smiled, and Imaan gave Hamid an approving nod.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 104458),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 122626),
          text:
              'Later, Hamid admitted, “I think I made him angry.” Imaan replied, “You gave everyone the same standard. That is respect too – giving people their rights even when you do not benefit from it.” Rashid added, “Doing the right thing can cost someone’s approval.”',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 123183),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 135834),
          text:
              'Akhlaaq said, “And this time we did not choose the answer first and invent reasons afterwards.” Hamid laughed. “You really are proud of those criteria.” “Very,” Akhlaaq replied.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 150802),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 161042),
          text:
              'Imaan added gently, “The Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him) taught that Allah does not look at our appearance or wealth, but at our hearts and deeds. What matters is not how important we look to people, but what kind of person we are becoming.” (Reference: Sahih Muslim 2564c.)',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 162162),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 184850),
          text:
              'That evening, Hamid’s friend confronted him near the school gate. “You have changed.” Hamid looked at him. “Maybe that is because I was doing some things wrong before.” His friend shook his head. “You are becoming just like Rashid.” Hamid smiled slightly. “Maybe that is not such a bad thing.”',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 185509),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 195138),
          text:
              'Faasid watched from behind the gate. Hamid was beginning to recognise the direction of his advice before following it. That made Faasid’s tricks much weaker.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 195138),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 213706),
          text:
              'The next morning, Rashid found another note near the gate: “Sometimes the hardest person to respect is the one who has hurt your pride. Choose respect anyway.” The green dot remained in the corner.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 214906),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 220206),
          text: 'Challenge of the Day 8: Choose by Merit, Not Friendship',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 221206),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 233674),
          text:
              'Mission: When choosing teams, roles or helpers, use a fair reason – effort, skill, turn-taking or suitability – instead of choosing only your closest friends.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 233674),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 244547),
          text:
              'Level-Up: Ages 9-12: Before a group choice, agree on the criteria first. Then apply the same criteria to everyone.',
        ),
      ],
    ),
    Story(
      id: 'respect_en_09',
      title: 'Ep 9 · The Choice That Changed Everything',
      narrator: 'Imaan & Akhlaq',
      coverAsset: 'assets/covers/respect.webp',
      audioAsset:
          'assets/audio/en/respect/09_the_choice_that_changed_everything.ogg',
      category: StoryCategory.moral,
      description: 'Respect — episode 9 of 10.',
      captions: [
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 1853),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 6095),
          text: 'Chapter Nine: The Choice That Changed Everything',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 6095),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 23335),
          text:
              'The final week of the Spring Fair arrived with more excitement than anyone could handle. The classroom was filled with decorations, posters, ribbons, and enough glitter to make even the teachers nervous. Hamid stood in the middle of the room, looking around. "Everyone, we have a problem."',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 23335),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 27063),
          text: 'Rashid immediately became alert. "What happened?"',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 27063),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 32383),
          text:
              'Hamid pointed toward the decorations. "My friend has put glitter everywhere."',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 32383),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 38143),
          text:
              'His friend said, "It\'s called decoration." "It\'s called a mess," Hamid replied.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 38690),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 51259),
          text:
              'Akhlaaq looked around. “Hamid is right about the mess.” Hamid’s friend stared at him. “Whose side are you on?” Akhlaaq smiled. “The side of cleanliness – and the side that plans to finish before Maghrib.”',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 52391),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 54991),
          text: 'Akhlaaq smiled. "The side of cleanliness."',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 54991),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 61231),
          text:
              'For once, Hamid was not worried about looking important. He simply wanted the work done properly.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 61914),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 78799),
          text:
              '"Alright," "Let\'s finish this together." Everyone began working. Even Hamid’s friend helped for a while. But after some time, he became annoyed. "This is boring." He dropped his scissors on the desk. "I\'m leaving." Hamid looked at him. "We\'re not finished."',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 78799),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 82519),
          text: '"Then finish it yourself." He turned toward the door.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 82519),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 85967),
          text: 'Hamid stood up. "Wait." His friend stopped. "What?"',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 85967),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 100015),
          text:
              'Hamid took a breath. "I used to think being captain meant telling everyone what to do. Then I thought it meant getting special treatment. Now I think it means being responsible for what happens to everyone."',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 100190),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 105527),
          text:
              'Hamid continued, "You don\'t have to agree with me. But I can\'t keep following you just because you\'re my friend."',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 105935),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 109895),
          text: 'The room became silent. His friend\'s face changed.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 109895),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 117295),
          text:
              '"So you\'re choosing them over me?" Hamid looked at them. "No. I\'m choosing what\'s right."',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 117295),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 121295),
          text: 'His friend shook his head. "You have become boring."',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 121295),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 128463),
          text:
              'Hamid smiled. "Maybe." Then he picked up the scissors. "But at least I\'m not pretending anymore."',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 128463),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 148831),
          text:
              'The classroom stayed silent for a few seconds. Akhlaaq felt the urge to turn the serious moment into a joke, but he stopped. Hamid had just made a difficult choice, and respect sometimes meant allowing a serious moment to remain serious. Akhlaaq simply nodded. “Good choice.”',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 149506),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 163351),
          text:
              'Rashid smiled, and Imaan said, “You chose what was right even though it may cost you a friendship for a while.” Hamid exhaled. The room returned to work without applause, and somehow that felt more sincere.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 163351),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 170239),
          text:
              'Everyone laughed. Even Hamid. But behind his laughter was something new. It was relief.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 170239),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 177279),
          text:
              'He had finally made a choice for himself. Finally, he had chosen what he believed was right.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 177279),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 190567),
          text:
              'That evening, Rashid told his grandmother everything. She listened, then said, “Sometimes becoming a better person means stepping away from the company that keeps pulling you towards worse choices.”',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 190567),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 192367),
          text: '"I think he\'s happy."',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 192367),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 195727),
          text: '"Then perhaps he has learned something important."',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 195727),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 197847),
          text: '"What?" Rashid asked.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 197847),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 210015),
          text:
              'His grandmother became serious. “Remember, Rashid. The battle between good and bad does not always happen outside us. Sometimes it happens quietly inside our own hearts.”',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 210015),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 215295),
          text:
              'Rashid nodded. “And the direction changes when we choose which voice to follow.”',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 215295),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 229743),
          text:
              'The next morning, Hamid walked into the classroom. He was not wearing his captain badge. Instead, he was carrying a box of decorations. Rashid stared at him. "Where\'s your badge?"',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 229743),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 233743),
          text: 'Hamid looked down. "I forgot it."',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 234743),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 237743),
          text:
              'Akhlaaq smiled. "I think you have finally realised you don\'t need it to be useful."',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 237743),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 245743),
          text:
              'Hamid smiled. "Maybe." He placed the box on the desk. Then he turned toward Rashid.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 245743),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 245744),
          text: '"Rashid?"',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 245744),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 246071),
          text: '"Yes?"',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 246071),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 248351),
          text: '"Can I ask you something?"',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 248351),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 250511),
          text: '"Of course," Rashid replied.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 250511),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 255951),
          text:
              'Hamid hesitated. "Do you think... I could work with you three?"',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 255951),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 261151),
          text:
              'Rashid was surprised. "You brought decorations. That\'s a good start."',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 261151),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 262431),
          text: 'Hamid laughed.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 262802),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 276128),
          text:
              'Imaan and Akhlaaq said together, “Welcome.” Hamid laughed. “You two planned that, didn’t you?” “No,” Akhlaaq said. “But I listened long enough to let Imaan finish at the same time.” Imaan laughed.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 276128),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 279394),
          text: 'Hamid looked at all three of them. "Thanks."',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 279394),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 292767),
          text:
              'For the first time, he felt that he had not joined a group because it was popular. He had joined because it was good. And that felt much better. Across the classroom, Faasid stood silently.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 292767),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 300167),
          text:
              'Hamid looked at him. Faasid smiled weakly. "Well?" Hamid crossed his arms. "What?"',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 300647),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 304447),
          text:
              'Faasid pointed toward the group. "You\'re really choosing them?"',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 304447),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 306615),
          text: 'Hamid smiled. "Yes."',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 306615),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 307615),
          text: '"Why?"',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 307615),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 313615),
          text:
              'Hamid thought for a moment. "Because they remind me who I want to become."',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 314198),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 315615),
          text: 'Faasid\'s smile disappeared.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 316154),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 323615),
          text:
              'Faasid stepped backwards, caught his heel on a decoration box and knocked a loose packet of glitter over his own hair and shoulders.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 325583),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 335423),
          text:
              'Akhlaaq’s first instinct was to laugh. He did smile, but then remembered the younger boy behind the library. He picked up a cloth and held it out. “Here. You have glitter in your hair.”',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 336007),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 349151),
          text:
              'Faasid stared at him. “You are not going to make fun of me?” “I was tempted,” Akhlaaq admitted. “But I am trying to improve.” Hamid laughed softly. “Mashaa Allah. The miracle continues.”',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 349524),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 363551),
          text:
              'Faasid snatched the cloth, muttered something under his breath and stormed away. Akhlaaq watched him go. Humour had not disappeared from him; he was simply learning that another person’s embarrassment did not have to become his entertainment.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 364026),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 368879),
          text: 'Faasid stormed away. Hamid watched him leave. Then he smiled.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 368879),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 382839),
          text:
              'That afternoon, Rashid found another note at the gate: "When you choose what is right even when no one is watching, your character becomes your greatest reward." The familiar green dot sat below the last word.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 382839),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 388324),
          text:
              'Rashid held the note up. "I think the writer is finally going to make a mistake."',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 388932),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 396502),
          text:
              'Akhlaaq grinned. "Or maybe the mystery is waiting for us to notice the mistake it already made."',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 396502),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 400834),
          text: 'Challenge of the Day 9: Choose Your Own Voice',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 400834),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 416415),
          text:
              'Mission: When a friend pressures you to mock, exclude, cheat or avoid responsibility, pause for five seconds before answering. Ask yourself: \'Will I respect this choice tomorrow?\'',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 417535),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 425335),
          text:
              'Level-Up: Ages 9-12: Write one example of healthy friendship and one example of pressure that should be resisted.',
        ),
      ],
    ),
    Story(
      id: 'respect_en_10',
      title: 'Ep 10 · The Man Behind the Notes',
      narrator: 'Imaan & Akhlaq',
      coverAsset: 'assets/covers/respect.webp',
      audioAsset: 'assets/audio/en/respect/10_the_man_behind_the_notes.ogg',
      category: StoryCategory.moral,
      description: 'Respect — episode 10 of 10.',
      captions: [
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 49),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 3488),
          text: 'Chapter Ten: The Man Behind the Notes',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 3488),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 24671),
          text:
              'The day of the Spring Festival finally arrived. The school was brighter than it had ever looked. Colourful banners hung across the walls. Tables were covered with handmade crafts, games, books, and delicious food. Children ran from one activity to another while teachers tried their best to keep everything organised.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 26097),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 44984),
          text:
              'And right in the middle of it all stood Hamid. But he was not standing there like a king. He was working. He helped a younger child find his mother. He carried chairs for teachers. He helped kids arrange the games stall and even gave his previous friend a job when he awkwardly returned to the group.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 44985),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 50762),
          text: '"You\'re back," Hamid said. He looked at him. "Can I help?"',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 50762),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 52984),
          text: 'Hamid nodded. "Sure."',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 52985),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 58985),
          text:
              'It was a small moment. But it was also a beginning. Rashid watched them from a distance.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 60333),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 75953),
          text:
              'Imaan stood beside Rashid. “Look at him,” she said. “Our captain has finally become a captain.” Akhlaaq joined them carrying two chairs. “And I have learned not to interrupt the announcement about it.” Rashid laughed.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 79921),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 80388),
          text:
              'Later that afternoon, Sheikh Uncle gathered the students together near the stage.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 80388),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 85441),
          text:
              'He looked at Hamid. "I have been noticing your choices for some time."',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 85441),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 91471),
          text:
              'Hamid became nervous. "Is that good or bad?" Sheikh Uncle smiled.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 91471),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 95357),
          text: '"That depends on the day." The children laughed.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 95813),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 123857),
          text:
              'Sheikh Uncle continued, “A person can receive a title in one day, but earning trust and respect takes longer. Hamid, you learned that leadership is service. Rashid, you learned that disappointment does not excuse disrespect. Imaan, you learned that a correct message can still be delivered in a kinder way. Akhlaaq, you learned that listening – and knowing when not to joke – can be a form of respect too.”',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 123857),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 131856),
          text:
              'The four children exchanged looks. None of them had learned exactly the same lesson, but all of them had changed.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 132179),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 140825),
          text:
              'Sheikh Uncle looked at the wider group. “Respect is not about being feared, being the loudest person in the room or standing above everyone else.”',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 140825),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 156705),
          text:
              '“It is shown when we greet people, listen properly, protect dignity, keep our promises, use authority fairly, include those who are left out and correct wrong without humiliating the person who is wrong.”',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 156705),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 158745),
          text: 'Rashid smiled.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 158745),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 171992),
          text:
              'He added, “The Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him) showed through his character that dignity, mercy, humility and fairness are not decorations. They are ways of living.”',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 171993),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 176892),
          text:
              'He looked at the children. "That is the kind of respect that lasts."',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 176892),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 181761),
          text: 'The children listened quietly. Then Hamid raised his hand.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 181761),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 184670),
          text: 'Sheikh Uncle smiled. "Yes, Captain?"',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 184670),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 191761),
          text:
              'Hamid stood up. "I think I finally understand why Rashid didn\'t become angry when I won the election."',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 191761),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 199760),
          text:
              'Rashid looked surprised. Hamid continued, "He respected me even when I didn\'t always deserve it."',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 200439),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 205968),
          text:
              'Rashid smiled. "Everyone deserves basic dignity, even when their behaviour needs correcting."',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 205968),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 212189),
          text:
              'Hamid nodded. "And now I understand that respect is not something you win with votes."',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 212189),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 214929),
          text: 'Sheikh Uncle smiled. "Exactly."',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 214929),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 219568),
          text:
              'After the Spring Festival ended, the students began cleaning the school grounds.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 219697),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 232897),
          text:
              'After the Spring Festival ended, Rashid, Imaan, Akhlaaq and Hamid were cleaning the school grounds together when Rashid suddenly remembered the notes. “We still do not know who wrote them.”',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 232897),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 237097),
          text: 'Everyone stopped. "What about them?" Imaan asked.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 237097),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 250165),
          text:
              'Rashid pulled out the small collection he had carefully kept. "There have been so many. The first one at the school gate. Then the others. Whoever wrote them always seemed to know exactly what was happening."',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 250634),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 259633),
          text:
              'Akhlaaq spread the notes across a table. “Same paper. Same fold. Same green dot. Whoever wrote them likes consistency.”',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 259633),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 269352),
          text:
              'Just then, Sheikh Uncle stopped beside the cleanup table to sign the volunteer register. He uncapped a fountain pen. Green ink flowed across the page.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 269352),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 276593),
          text:
              'Rashid froze. He pulled the oldest note from his pocket and looked at the tiny green dot in its corner.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 276593),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 282182),
          text:
              'Green ink. Green dot. Rashid looked from the note to Sheikh Uncle\'s pen.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 282182),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 283753),
          text: 'His eyes widened.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 283753),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 285100),
          text: '"Wait... was it you?"',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 285661),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 287659),
          text: 'Sheikh Uncle asked, “Was what me?”',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 287659),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 293041),
          text:
              '“The notes,” Rashid said, suddenly less certain of his dramatic accusation.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 293841),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 295412),
          text: 'All looked confused.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 295412),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 299200),
          text: '"What notes?" Rashid quickly explained everything.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 299569),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 303136),
          text: 'Sheikh Uncle remained silent. Then he smiled. "Ah."',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 303136),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 307569),
          text: 'Rashid pointed at him. "That \'ah\' sounded suspicious."',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 308565),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 311969),
          text: 'Sheikh Uncle laughed. "I suppose I have been discovered."',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 312416),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 315545),
          text: 'Imaan stared at him. “You wrote all of them?”',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 316104),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 317668),
          text: 'Sheikh Uncle nodded. "Yes."',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 317668),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 319568),
          text: 'Hamid stared. "But why?"',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 321045),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 335417),
          text:
              'Sheikh Uncle looked at Rashid. "Because I was paying attention. You were disappointed after the election, but you kept trying to do the right thing. I wanted to encourage you without turning good character into a performance for praise."',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 335417),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 341025),
          text:
              'Rashid blinked. "But how did you know what happened in places you were not even standing?"',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 341575),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 357025),
          text:
              'Sheikh Uncle smiled. “I did not know everything. The younger boy Rashid defended came to my bookshop and told me what happened. Miss mentioned the cleanup group. Students told me when Hamid began changing. I never asked anyone to spy on you.”',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 357025),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 366229),
          text:
              '"So the whole school was your news network?" Rashid asked. "Only the very talkative part of it," Sheikh Uncle replied, and the children laughed.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 367392),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 377860),
          text:
              'Hamid looked at the collection of notes. Then he became quiet. "So all this time," he said slowly, "you were trying to encourage Rashid?"',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 377860),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 379846),
          text: 'Sheikh Uncle nodded. "Yes."',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 379846),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 384041),
          text: 'Hamid looked at Rashid. "Even when I won?"',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 384041),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 395041),
          text:
              'Sheikh Uncle smiled and said, "Because sometimes the person who loses needs encouragement. But sometimes the person who wins needs guidance even more."',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 395041),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 408809),
          text:
              'Hamid lowered his eyes. The election had given him a title, but Rashid\'s behaviour had shown him a different kind of respect – the kind that cannot be demanded. Hamid turned to Rashid. "I am sorry."',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 408809),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 411049),
          text: 'Rashid smiled. "For what?"',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 411049),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 417968),
          text:
              '"For acting as if the badge made me better than everyone else – and for the times I used it to pressure people."',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 417968),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 420377),
          text: 'Rashid held out his hand. "Friends?"',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 420377),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 423377),
          text: 'Hamid smiled and shook it. "Friends."',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 423777),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 436377),
          text:
              'Imaan and Akhlaaq smiled as the two boys shook hands. Imaan was glad Hamid had apologised without being publicly forced. Akhlaaq was even more glad that nobody had asked him to make a speech.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 436377),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 440922),
          text: 'And somewhere nearby, Faasid watched the scene.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 441553),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 451944),
          text:
              'Faasid, standing a little distance away, groaned. “Respect. Kindness. Good manners. Friendship. You people make everything difficult.”',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 452537),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 459344),
          text:
              'Akhlaaq looked at him. For half a second a joke formed in his mind. Then he simply said, “You could try some of it.” Hamid laughed.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 461062),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 472993),
          text:
              'Faasid rolled his eyes and walked away. This time nobody chased him and nobody needed to defeat him with a speech. Hamid had already made the choice that mattered.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 472993),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 487921),
          text:
              'As the sun began to set, Sheikh Uncle gathered the children near the gate. “The notes were never prizes,” he said. “They were reminders. Good character may go unnoticed for a while, but that does not make it less valuable.”',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 487921),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 497200),
          text:
              '"And respect is not permission to ignore bad behaviour," he added. "We can correct what is wrong without humiliating the person who is wrong."',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 497200),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 498121),
          text: 'Rashid nodded.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 499249),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 502769),
          text: '"People can also change," Hamid added quietly.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 502769),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 511889),
          text:
              'Sheikh Uncle smiled. “Exactly. Respect, mercy, honesty and humility are choices we practise every day.”',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 512332),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 515329),
          text: 'Imaan said, “Imaan should guide the heart.”',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 515776),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 519863),
          text:
              'Akhlaaq added, “And akhlaaq should show that guidance through our actions.”',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 520418),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 525777),
          text:
              'Rashid smiled. “Respect is one of the ways people can feel that guidance from us.”',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 527037),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 534119),
          text:
              'Sheikh Uncle smiled proudly. "Now you understand." Hamid looked at his captain badge. Then he removed it.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 534119),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 540185),
          text:
              'Everyone stared at him. "Don\'t worry," Hamid said quickly. "I\'m not quitting."',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 540185),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 543425),
          text: '"Then why did you take it off?" Rashid asked.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 543425),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 550785),
          text:
              'Hamid grinned. "Because I\'m going home. My mother says I have to take my school things off before dinner."',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 550785),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 561713),
          text:
              'As they began walking away, Ayaan called from the gate. “Wait!” He hurried towards them with a folded paper in his hand and a small blue star drawn in one corner.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 561713),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 578793),
          text:
              '“This is for you,” he said, handing it to Rashid, Imaan and Akhlaaq. Rashid opened it. The handwriting was uneven but careful: “Thank you for asking what I could do before deciding what I could not do. I am going to try to do the same for someone else.”',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 579121),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 587898),
          text:
              'For a moment, nobody spoke. Then Akhlaaq looked at the blue star and grinned. “So this one is not part of Sheikh Uncle’s mystery.”',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 588249),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 592684),
          text:
              'Ayaan shook his head. “No. I just liked the idea of leaving a good message.”',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 592991),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 605088),
          text:
              'Imaan folded the note carefully. “Then maybe the notes did what they were supposed to do.” Rashid nodded. Respect had not ended with the person who received it. It had moved forward.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 605089),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 613682),
          text:
              'Hamid looked towards Ayaan. “Inn shaa Allah, we keep it moving.” Ayaan smiled. “Inn shaa Allah.”',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 615089),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 619089),
          text: 'Challenge of the Day 10: The Respect Chain',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 620194),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 636057),
          text:
              'Mission: Complete three acts today: thank someone who serves or helps your family or school, include someone who is often left out, and apologise for one disrespectful action if you owe an apology.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 637744),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 652024),
          text:
              'Level-Up: Family finish: At dinner or before bed, let each family member share one way they want to show more respect this week. No one is allowed to mock another person\'s answer.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 652025),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 657025),
          text: 'Islamic Reference Notes for the Editor / Parent',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 657025),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 667793),
          text:
              'Human dignity and standing for a funeral procession: Sahih al-Bukhari 1312–1313.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 667793),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 676353),
          text:
              'The best people are those with the best character: Sahih al-Bukhari 6035.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 676353),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 681961),
          text:
              'Love for your brother what you love for yourself: Sahih al-Bukhari 13.',
        ),
        CaptionLine(
          start: Duration(milliseconds: 681961),
          end: Duration(milliseconds: 691961),
          text:
              'Allah looks at hearts and deeds rather than appearance or wealth: Sahih Muslim 2564c.',
        ),
      ],
    ),
  ],
);
